---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Architecture from '../../components/Architecture.astro';
import '../../styles/case.css';

import eventsDesktop from '../../assets/work/events-desktop.jpg';
import eventsPhone from '../../assets/work/events-phone.jpg';
---

<Base
  title="Realtime Events — case study · Darwin Herrera"
  description="Real-time event management where every open screen updates live while the database stays the single source of truth: a domain-first FastAPI service with WebSocket notifications and a React client."
  ogType="article"
  contactTitle="Need live features that stay consistent?"
  contactText="Tell me on LinkedIn what you are building. I answer every message."
  backToWork
>
  <section class="container case-hero">
    <h1>Keeping every screen in sync while people change the same events</h1>
    <p class="lede text-2">
      Realtime Events lets people create, join, leave, cancel and restore events while every open screen updates live,
      with the database as the single source of truth.
    </p>
    <dl class="case-meta">
      <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Sole engineer, backend and frontend</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Stack</dt><dd>FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, PostgreSQL, React, TypeScript</dd></div>
      <div>
        <dt>Live demo</dt>
        <dd><a href="https://tt-events-realtime-frontend-react.vercel.app">tt-events-realtime-frontend-react.vercel.app</a></dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Source</dt>
        <dd>
          <a href="https://github.com/darwinhc/tt-events-realtime.service.python">Backend</a> ·
          <a href="https://github.com/darwinhc/tt-events-realtime.frontend.react">Frontend</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <figure class="container case-figure">
    <Image
      src={eventsDesktop}
      alt="Realtime Events on a desktop: the event list with dates, places and attendee counts, and the selected event with its attendees"
      widths={[640, 960, 1200, 1600]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1264px) 1200px, 100vw"
      class="shot"
      style="width: 100%"
    />
    <figcaption class="small muted">
      The event list and the selected event, with demo data. A canceled event stays visible and can be restored by its
      organizer.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <article class="container split case-section flush-top">
    <h2 class="split-title">The problem</h2>
    <div class="split-body text-2">
      <p>
        Several people act on the same events at the same time: one edits, another joins, the organizer cancels. Every
        open screen has to show the current state without a refresh, and no screen may show something the database does
        not hold.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual shortcut, pushing state over the socket and treating it as the truth, breaks the moment a message is
        lost or arrives out of order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container split case-section">
      <h2 class="split-title">How it works</h2>
      <div class="split-body text-2">
        <h3>The rules live in the domain</h3>
        <p>
          Event lifecycle, organizer permissions and joining rules sit in a pure domain layer. FastAPI only parses
          requests and calls use cases, so the rules are tested without a server.
        </p>
        <h3>HTTP is the source of truth</h3>
        <p>
          Every change goes through the REST API and is committed first. Only then does one WebSocket channel notify
          every open client, which maps the notification into its own state. A notification a client cannot read makes
          it reload from the API.
        </p>
        <h3>A written contract for the live channel</h3>
        <p>
          The WebSocket messages are specified in an AsyncAPI 3.1 document, so the frontend and any other client build
          against the same contract.
        </p>
        <h3>Honest about scale</h3>
        <p>
          The live channel runs inside one service instance. The documentation names the next step to scale it out, a
          shared broker such as Redis Pub/Sub or PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY, instead of pretending the limit is not there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container case-section case-arch" aria-labelledby="arch-title">
    <div class="split">
      <h2 id="arch-title" class="split-title">Architecture</h2>
      <p class="split-body text-2">
        Ports and adapters. FastAPI is only the entry point; use cases work over the domain, and persistence, the live
        channel and migrations are adapters. SQLite runs locally and PostgreSQL in production.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Architecture
      summary="Realtime Events architecture: the React app and every other open client call the FastAPI service over REST and receive WebSocket notifications after each commit. Use cases work over a domain of event lifecycle, organizer permissions and joining rules. Adapters implement the ports with PostgreSQL through SQLAlchemy, a realtime publisher and Alembic migrations."
      clients={[
        { icon: 'si:react', title: 'React app', detail: 'TypeScript · Vite · on Vercel' },
        { icon: 'lucide:users', title: 'Every other open client', detail: 'Same event list, live' },
      ]}
      clientLink="REST in, WebSocket notification out after each commit"
      twoWay
      entry={{ icon: 'si:fastapi', title: 'FastAPI', detail: 'REST /events, /joiners · WebSocket /ws/events' }}
      useCases={['Create', 'Update', 'Cancel and restore', 'Join', 'Leave']}
      domain={['Event lifecycle', 'Organizer permissions', 'Joining rules']}
      adapters={[
        { icon: 'si:postgresql', title: 'PostgreSQL', detail: 'SQLAlchemy repositories · Supabase in production' },
        { icon: 'lucide:radio-tower', title: 'Realtime publisher', detail: 'Notifies clients after each commit' },
        { icon: 'lucide:history', title: 'Alembic migrations', detail: 'Versioned schema changes' },
      ]}
      adapterLink="Through ports the core defines"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="case-dark" aria-labelledby="phone-title">
    <div class="container inner">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="phone-title">The same events on a phone</h2>
        <p>
          A split list and detail view on desktop becomes one list on a phone. Joined and canceled events are marked,
          and the interface switches between English and German.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="media">
        <Image
          src={eventsPhone}
          alt="Realtime Events on a phone: the event list with joined and canceled events marked"
          widths={[260, 520]}
          sizes="260px"
          class="phone"
          style="width: min(260px, 70%); border-radius: 24px"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container split case-section">
    <h2 class="split-title">Result</h2>
    <div class="split-body text-2">
      <p>
        The service runs in production with a public demo: the frontend on Vercel, the API on Render and PostgreSQL
        behind Alembic migrations.
      </p>
      <p>
        The backend has <strong>100+ automated tests with coverage above 85%</strong>, enforced in CI, and the frontend
        keeps UI components apart from API and WebSocket logic, with its own unit tests.
      </p>
      <p class="tech-line">
        Python · FastAPI · SQLAlchemy · Alembic · PostgreSQL · WebSockets · AsyncAPI · React · TypeScript · Vite ·
        Tailwind
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
